;; SEARCHING A STRING TREE WITHOUT MATERIALISING IT.
;;
;; `DECISIONS.md#strings-and-matching` names this operation in the list of things that must
;; use the structure rather than flatten:
;;
;;   `index-of`, `split`, `replace`, comparison -- walk leaves through the
;;   cursor. None of them needs contiguous bytes.
;;
;; and then predicts, in the next paragraph, exactly what was here instead:
;;
;;   A rope that flattens on every `index-of` passes every correctness test
;;   and is slower than the flat string it replaced.
;;
;; It did. Native called `string_arg` on the HAYSTACK, which flattens the tree
;; into a flat string, caches it and charges for the copy. Measured on a 3.5 KB
;; rope: 897 steps for the first search and 26 for the second, against 21 and
;; 26 on the JVM, which never flattened at all. The gap was not a mispriced
;; charge -- it was a copy one runtime made and the others did not.
;;
;; WHERE THE BAD JUSTIFICATION CAME FROM, because it was not carelessness.
;; `strings-and-matching` DOES conclude "flatten before matching" -- in the section about HOST
;; REGEX ENGINES, where .NET wants a `string`, JavaScript wants a `string` and
;; Rust's `regex` wants a `&str`. That is a real constraint and it is not this
;; operation's. A substring search needs no contiguous bytes, and the
;; conclusion was carried across to a function that never asked for it.
;;
;; AND THIS IS WHY THE THREE RUNTIMES DISAGREED ON GAS. There were three
;; implementations: Rust searched UTF-8 bytes off a flattened flint string; the
;; JVM built a `java.lang.String` and searched UTF-16; the CLR built a .NET
;; `string`, searched UTF-16, and then allocated `h.Substring(0, i)` purely to
;; count code points. Three algorithms over three representations, agreeing on
;; the answer and on nothing else. One source is the only fix that makes gas
;; agree BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by patching three charge sites.
;;
;; THE NEEDLE IS MADE CONTIGUOUS AND THE HAYSTACK IS NOT, which is the whole
;; distinction. A needle is bounded by the pattern the caller is looking for; a
;; haystack is bounded by the data. Flattening the first is a copy of something
;; small and known, and it is the same trade `strings-and-matching` already accepts when a
;; small slice of a large rope copies down into flat.
;;
;; ONE PATH, NOT TWO. Native had an ASCII branch over bytes and a non-ASCII
;; branch that built a `&str` and counted `chars()`. Counting code-point STARTS
;; -- every byte that is not a continuation byte -- is correct for both and
;; needs neither branch, so the ASCII/non-ASCII split is gone with the flatten.
(ns flint.rt.ropefind
  (:require [flint.impl.rt :refer [defn return if let do comment doc == != < >
                                   >= not nil? + - bit-and local set forever
                                   break continue while <= leaf-len leaf-byte
                                   leaf-find
                                   walk-open
                                   walk-close walk-dup walk-next charge-tick
                                   charge-work to-i64 fixnum and is-heap
                                   as-heap to-addr run-eq Rt Value I32 Bool
                                   Walk NIL STR_DATA]]
            [flint.rt.ropemeas :refer [is-rope s-count s-ascii]]
            [flint.rt.ropecp :refer [rope-byte-of-cp]]
            [flint.rt.ropeflat :refer [s-flatten]]))

(doc "Do the `nn` bytes of `nd` sit at (`leaf0`, `p0`) and onward?"
     ""
     "A DUPLICATE CURSOR, which is what `walk-dup` exists for -- `tree-eq`"
     "uses it to peek the other side. The match may run off the end of the"
     "current leaf and into the next ones, and the outer walk must not move"
     "while it does, because a failed match resumes at the very next byte."
     ""
     "CHARGED PER BYTE COMPARED. The outer loop's `charge-tick` is what STOPS"
     "a scan that cannot be paid for; this bills the work the match itself"
     "does, which is bounded by the needle and would otherwise be the same"
     "kind of hole a collision scan was.")
(defn ^:method ^Bool match-at [^:mut ^Rt rt ^Walk wh ^Value leaf0 ^I32 p0
                               ^Value nd ^I32 nn]
  (comment "THE WHOLE NEEDLE INSIDE ONE LEAF, WITH NO CURSOR AT ALL, which is"
           "almost every candidate and was the cost of the whole search."
           "`walk-dup` CLONES the cursor's stack, so taking one per candidate"
           "allocated once per position where the first byte matched --"
           "searching 34 KB for a needle starting with `p`, in a haystack of"
           "`piece0-piece1-...`, is four thousand clones to answer four"
           "thousand comparisons that never leave the leaf they started in."
           ""
           "So the boundary-crossing cursor below is now the RARE path, taken"
           "only when the match would actually run off the end of this leaf.")
  (if (<= (+ p0 nn) (leaf-len rt leaf0))
    (do
      (local ^:mut ^I32 k)
      (local ^:mut ^Bool hit)
      (set k 0)
      (set hit true)
      (comment "A MEMCMP WHEN BOTH SIDES HAVE AN ADDRESS, which is what"
               "`run-eq` is for and what `tree-eq` already does one file"
               "along. The byte loop below was ~14 `leaf-byte` calls per"
               "candidate -- searching 34 KB of `piece0-piece1-...` for a"
               "needle starting with `p` means four thousand candidates, so"
               "fifty-six thousand calls through the tier branch, and that"
               "was the search."
               ""
               "AN INLINE LEAF HAS NO ADDRESS, so a pair with one on either"
               "side still goes a byte at a time -- at most seven bytes, and"
               "rare.")
      (if (and (is-heap leaf0) (is-heap nd))
        (do
          (charge-work rt (to-i64 nn))
          (return (run-eq rt (+ (+ (as-heap leaf0) STR_DATA) (to-addr p0))
                          (+ (as-heap nd) STR_DATA)
                          nn))))
      (while (< k nn)
        (do
          (comment "CHARGED PER BYTE COMPARED, exactly as the cursor path"
                   "below does. Billing `nn` flat would be simpler and would"
                   "over-charge every early mismatch, which is safe for a"
                   "budget and untrue about the work -- and `resource-limits` asks for"
                   "the second thing.")
          (charge-work rt 1)
          (if (!= (leaf-byte rt leaf0 (+ p0 k)) (leaf-byte rt nd k))
            (do (set hit false)
                (set k nn))
            (set k (+ k 1)))))
      (return hit)))
  (let [^Walk wd (walk-dup rt wh)]
    (local ^:mut ^Value ml)
    (local ^:mut ^I32 q)
    (local ^:mut ^I32 m)
    (local ^:mut ^I32 j)
    (local ^:mut ^Bool ok)
    (set ml leaf0)
    (set q p0)
    (set m (leaf-len rt leaf0))
    (set j 0)
    (set ok true)
    (forever
      (if (== j nn)
        (break))
      (if (== q m)
        (do
          (let [^Value v (walk-next rt wd)]
            (if (nil? rt v)
              (do (set ok false)
                  (break)))
            (set ml v)
            (set q 0)
            (set m (leaf-len rt v))
            (continue))))
      (charge-work rt 1)
      (if (!= (leaf-byte rt ml q) (leaf-byte rt nd j))
        (do (set ok false)
            (break)))
      (set q (+ q 1))
      (set j (+ j 1)))
    (walk-close rt wd)
    (return ok)))

(doc "The CODE-POINT index of `needle` in `hay` at or after code point"
     "`from`, as a fixnum, or NIL when it is not there."
     ""
     "A VALUE RATHER THAN AN INT, because `I32` is UNSIGNED on all three"
     "targets: there is no -1 to return, and the first version of this tried"
     "to. A fixnum-or-nil is also exactly what the three builtins want to hand"
     "back, so the conversion disappears rather than moving."
     ""
     "`from` IS CLAMPED BY THE CALLER for the same reason -- a negative"
     "`from` arrives here as a very large number, so the builtins do the"
     "`max(from, 0)` where the sign still exists."
     ""
     "NOTHING ALLOCATES ONCE THE WALK IS OPEN. The needle is flattened first,"
     "before `walk-open`, so every read after that is a byte out of a leaf"
     "that cannot move -- which is why no leaf here is rooted (`a-vec-of-values-is-not-a-root`).")
(defn ^:pub ^:method ^Value s-index-of
  [^:mut ^Rt rt ^Value hay ^Value needle ^I32 from]
  (comment "A LOCAL AND A `set`, because kin's `if` is a statement: it cannot"
           "sit in a `let` binding, which is what the first version tried.")
  (local ^:mut ^Value nd)
  (local ^:mut ^I32 at)
  (set nd needle)
  (if (is-rope rt needle)
    (set nd (s-flatten rt needle)))
  (let [^I32 skip from
        ^I32 nn (leaf-len rt nd)]
    (comment "AN EMPTY NEEDLE IS FOUND WHERE THE SEARCH STARTS, CLAMPED TO"
             "THE END -- `(str/index-of \"abc\" \"\" 100)` is 3 in Clojure, not"
             "nil, because Java's `indexOf` answers the length when the needle"
             "is empty and `from` is past it."
             ""
             "THE THREE RUNTIMES DISAGREED HERE and nothing caught it. Native's"
             "ASCII path returned NIL, native's non-ASCII path returned the"
             "count, and both ports returned the count -- so the answer"
             "depended on whether the haystack happened to be ASCII, and"
             "native contradicted its own ports for the ASCII half. Found by"
             "differencing 1 560 cases against the old implementations; the"
             "conformance probes had never asked.")
    (if (== nn 0)
      (do
        (set at skip)
        (if (> at (s-count rt hay))
          (set at (s-count rt hay)))
        (return (fixnum (to-i64 at)))))
    (comment "SEEK TO `from` BY DESCENT, NOT BY SCANNING, which is what made"
             "`str/split` quadratic the moment this stopped flattening. Each"
             "successive `index-of` starts further in, and a walk that opens"
             "at the root and scans forward re-reads the whole prefix every"
             "time: `test/scaling.clj` measured 3.56x for a doubled input"
             "where linear is 2x, and caught it."
             ""
             "`rope-byte-of-cp` answers the byte offset in O(depth) using the"
             "per-node code-point counts `strings-and-matching` created for this and which,"
             "as `ropecp` notes, nothing used because every path flattened"
             "first. Leaves entirely before that offset are then skipped by"
             "LENGTH -- one `leaf-len` each, no bytes read.")
    (let [^Walk wh (walk-open rt hay)
          ^Bool ascii (s-ascii rt hay)
          ^I32 startbyte (rope-byte-of-cp rt hay skip)]
      (local ^:mut ^I32 base)
      (set base 0)
      (local ^:mut ^Value leaf)
      (local ^:mut ^I32 p)
      (local ^:mut ^I32 n)
      (local ^:mut ^I32 cp)
      (local ^:mut ^Value out)
      (local ^:mut ^I32 ticks)
      (set leaf NIL)
      (set p 0)
      (set n 0)
      (set cp 0)
      (set out NIL)
      (set ticks 0)
      (forever
        (if (== p n)
          (do
            (let [^Value v (walk-next rt wh)]
              (if (nil? rt v)
                (break))
              (set base (+ base n))
              (set leaf v)
              (set p 0)
              (set n (leaf-len rt v))
              (comment "WHOLLY BEFORE THE START: skipped by length, without"
                       "reading a byte of it.")
              (if (<= (+ base n) startbyte)
                (do (set p n)
                    (continue)))
              (comment "THE LEAF THAT CONTAINS IT: jump in, and the code-point"
                       "index is `skip` by construction -- that is what"
                       "`rope-byte-of-cp` was asked."
                       ""
                       "`<=` AND NOT `<`. A leaf that begins exactly AT the"
                       "seek offset needs no jump but does need `cp` set, and"
                       "with `<` it did not get one -- the walk resumed"
                       "believing it was at code point zero, and `from=1` on"
                       "`abcabc` answered 2 instead of 3. Later leaves have"
                       "`base > startbyte`, so this fires once.")
              (if (<= base startbyte)
                (do (set p (- startbyte base))
                    (set cp skip)))
              (continue))))
        (comment "BOUNDED HERE. A haystack is as long as the data says, so the"
                 "check has to be inside the walk rather than a pre-charge"
                 "against a length that is known but arbitrary.")
        (comment "THE TICK CHECKS AND DOES NOT CHARGE -- `charge-work` below"
                 "bills the bytes. Ticking once per LEAF and charging there"
                 "made the same bytes cost different gas in different shapes:"
                 "a three-leaf rope ticked three times where a flat string"
                 "ticked once, and the probe printed `gas=4,5` for what had"
                 "been `6,6`. The BYTES are what the work is, and they do not"
                 "depend on how the tree was built.")
        (if (not (charge-tick rt (to-i64 ticks) 0 "str-index-of"))
          (break))
        (set ticks (+ ticks 1))
        (comment "THE SKIP. Every byte between here and the next occurrence of"
                 "the needle's first byte can start no match, but each one"
                 "still has to be COUNTED if it is a code-point start. So the"
                 "run is found in one call and then counted, rather than"
                 "tested one byte at a time -- which is the difference between"
                 "a memchr and a call per byte, and was six times the runtime"
                 "of the flatten this replaced.")
        (let [^I32 hit (leaf-find rt leaf (leaf-byte rt nd 0) p)]
          (charge-work rt (to-i64 (- hit p)))
          (comment "ASCII SKIPS THE COUNTING TOO, and this is most of the"
                   "cost. `leaf-find` jumps over a run in one call, and then"
                   "the run still had to be walked BYTE BY BYTE to count code"
                   "points -- 34 KB of flat ASCII took 142us, 4.2ns a byte,"
                   "for a scan that should be a memchr. When every byte is a"
                   "code point the count IS the distance, and the header"
                   "already knows (`s-ascii`, set once when the string was"
                   "built).")
          (if ascii
            (do
              (set cp (+ cp (- hit p)))
              (set p hit))
            (while (< p hit)
              (do
                (if (!= (bit-and (leaf-byte rt leaf p) 192) 128)
                  (set cp (+ cp 1)))
                (set p (+ p 1)))))
          (if (< p n)
            (do
              (charge-work rt 1)
              (comment "A CONTINUATION BYTE IS 10xxxxxx and starts nothing, so"
                       "it advances the byte position and not the code-point"
                       "index. This is the whole of the multi-byte handling.")
              (if (!= (bit-and (leaf-byte rt leaf p) 192) 128)
                (do
                  (if (>= cp skip)
                    (if (match-at rt wh leaf p nd nn)
                      (do (set out (fixnum (to-i64 cp)))
                          (break))))
                  (set cp (+ cp 1))))
              (set p (+ p 1))))))
      (walk-close rt wh)
      (return out))))
